+
+## What the fleet should read out of this
+
+1. **LLM06 is the fleet's center of mass** — policy engine, fail-closed approvals, HITL signing, admin quarantine, RBAC, tenancy. That is the right place to be strong for an agent-fleet product, and the row to protect against regressions first.
+2. **The two cheapest upgrades are roster lines, not code:** `prompt_injection_tester.py` and `ai_memory_integrity_audit.py` already exist; adding them to `run_all_guards.py` (or a scheduled pass) converts LLM01/LLM04 PARTIALs toward ENFORCED without new logic. That is a maintainer decision (roster semantics, runtime cost) — deliberately not bundled into this docs-only lane.
+3. **LLM10 is not hypothetical** — #1698 showed the class live in production within 24 hours of this taxonomy being mapped. The standing gap (failover-chain burn-rate telemetry) belongs to the platform-guardian cadence, not to this file.
+4. **Filed follow-ups already cover two gaps:** F2 (`.agents/` outside bandit scan surface) and F4 (credential-gated skill verdict layer) from the R26 baseline; #1747 and #1748 carry the LLM08/LLM09 depth work.
